  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Addressing the assembled staff in the hotel lobby, Eleonora Andreevna calls yesterday's opening in front of the Prefect a complete failure and demands to know why Chef Barinov is absent. Listen to her speech.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вчерашнее открытие — это полный провал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это случилось при самом префекте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не буду это терпеть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Какая строгая у нас хозяйка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это касается здесь каждого.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Главное в отеле — это дисциплина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дисциплина и ещё раз дисциплина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надеюсь, мы избежим таких разговоров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А где сейчас ваш шеф?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он очень перед вами извинялся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он решает проблемы с поставщиками.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Где же сейчас наш шеф?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вчерашнее открытие — это полный провал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yesterday's grand opening was a total failure.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'yesterday's' and finishing on 'failure'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это случилось при самом префекте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This happened in front of the prefect himself.' Notice the reflexive suffix on the verb meaning 'happened', turning the action back on the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не буду это терпеть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am not going to tolerate this.' Eleonora brings out the contrast between 'I' and 'not' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Какая строгая у нас хозяйка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a strict owner we have.' In Russian, the idea of 'what a' flows naturally into 'landlady' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это касается здесь каждого.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This concerns everyone here.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'this' and finishing on 'every'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Главное в отеле — это дисциплина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The main thing in a hotel is discipline.' The grammar packs 'the main thing' and 'discipline' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дисциплина и ещё раз дисциплина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Discipline and once again discipline.' The meaning hinges on 'discipline' working together with 'time' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надеюсь, мы избежим таких разговоров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope we will avoid such conversations.' Notice how Russian expresses 'hope' and 'talks' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А где сейчас ваш шеф?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And where is your head chef right now?' The meaning hinges on 'and' working together with 'proper name' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он очень перед вами извинялся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He apologized to you profusely.' The grammar packs 'he' and 'apologized' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он решает проблемы с поставщиками.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is solving problems with suppliers.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'he' before landing on 'with suppliers'. Senya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Где же сейчас наш шеф?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is our chef right now, though?' Senya brings out the contrast between 'where' and 'but' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
